SPURS HLE and SPU runtime: workloads, wake-ups, thread images, diagnostics.

- cellSpurs: every taskset is its own workload (unique wid, bit in wklEnabled, taskset EA in wklInfo1[wid].arg), which
  is where a pushed LFQueue entry finds the task to signal (src/spurs_lfqueue.cpp).
- A task parked in WAIT_SIGNAL wakes on queue data (spu_channels, spu_workload).
- An SPU thread's lifted image is chosen by the content of its image (two images had code at the entry address), and
  an event sent through the interrupt mailbox waits a few ms for the PPU's connect_event that follows group_start.
- spu_register_ls_hook, SPU_GETLOG=<lo>,<hi> (log MFC transfers touching an LS range), lr in the "synthesised stop"
  message.
- The "if (_n++ < N)" log caps used signed counters that wrapped negative after 2^31 calls and then printed on
  every call (millions of "[spu] drain-resume" lines in battle); they saturate now (PS3_LOG_FIRST). Same for the SPURS, SPU and lv2
  syscall files.
- The WAIT_SIGNAL wait and wake share one lock/broadcast/timed-wait helper set (spu_workload.c), and the lookup of
  a thread's lifted image by content is one function (lv2_register.c).

